# backend_sweep_partialsb_2026-09-02 — the svt-rs PARTIAL-SUPERBLOCK envelope,
# measured because the docs describing it were backwards

Harness, pins, box and columns: identical to
`backend_sweep_2026-09-02.meta` — read that file first. Only the size axis
differs.

## What this dataset is for

`Av1Backend::SvtRs`'s rustdoc and the `encode-svt-rs` feature comment both
said dimensions must be multiples of 64 except at speed >= 5. The gate they
describe, `svt_rs_dims_error` (`src/encoder_svt_rs.rs:265`), says the
opposite for the colour path: a 4:2:0 colour image of ANY size is accepted at
EVERY speed, and only an alpha/grayscale Cs400 item at a non-multiple-of-64
size additionally needs speed >= 5 and multiples of 8. The prose was corrected
in `c284f99`; this dataset is the measurement behind that correction, so the
claim rests on encoded bitstreams rather than on a reading of the source.

## Grid

6 CID22-512 validation images x sizes {65, 100, 200, 333, 500} — **none a
multiple of 64, and 65 / 333 not multiples of 8 either** — x quality 5..=100
step 5 x speed 6 x arms {zenravif-420, zenravif-444, svt-rs-420}.
600 cells x 3 = 1800 rows.

## Result

    svt-rs-420 cells attempted:  600
    svt-rs-420 cells encoded:    600   (no dimension refusal, no failure)
    cross-decoder byte gate:    1800 / 1800 identical

So the colour path does code partial superblocks at every one of these
geometries, and both AV1 decoders agree bit-for-bit on the result.

Scope limit, stated rather than glossed: speed 6 maps to SVT preset 7
(`speed_to_svt_preset`), so this dataset alone does NOT exercise the claim
below preset 6 — the half that the 2026-08-29 floor removal was actually
about. That half is pinned by `svt_rs_partial_sb_roundtrip_at_low_presets`
(96x96 / 65x72 / 100x37 at speeds 1-4 = presets 0/1/3/4, decoded at the true
size), and the mono refusal that survives is pinned by
`svt_rs_mono_partial_sb_still_refused_below_preset_6`. A zenravif arm at
speed 1 over this grid would cost hours, which is why the low-preset evidence
stays in the test suite where it is cheap.

## RD and speed at these geometries

Bytes at matched ssim2 (log-interpolated per image, median across images),
svt-rs-420 / zenravif-420:

     size   ssim2 50   60      70      80      90
       65      1.035  0.936   1.069   0.929   0.851
      100      1.015  1.063   1.159   1.037   0.845
      200      1.061  1.083   1.104   1.120   1.053
      333      1.113  1.168   1.179   1.126   1.063
      500      1.135  1.173   1.183   1.127   1.040

Median encode wall time, and the speed ratio zenravif-420 / svt-rs-420:

     size   svt-rs   zenravif   ratio
       65   0.63 ms   13.81 ms   21.9x
      100   1.25 ms   34.13 ms   27.4x
      200   3.96 ms  112.85 ms   28.5x
      333  10.94 ms  330.65 ms   30.2x
      500  22.48 ms  739.17 ms   32.9x

The shape matches the aligned dataset: parity-to-slightly-worse bytes at the
small end, 3-18% more bytes from 200 up, and a 20-33x encode-time advantage.
There is no partial-superblock penalty visible in either axis — the odd-size
ratios sit inside the aligned dataset's range.

209 of 1800 rows have ssim2 < 20; all are quality 5 or 10 and they appear on
all three arms, i.e. that is the quality dial bottoming out, not corruption.
The corruption canary this repo cares about (ssim2 ~ -700 on QP 0, the
2026-07-22 finding) does not appear at all.

## Repro

    nice -n 19 ./target/release/examples/encode_backend_sweep \
      --corpus <codec-corpus>/CID22/CID22-512/validation \
      --images 6 --sizes 65,100,200,333,500 --qualities 5..=100:5 \
      --speeds 6 --threads 4 \
      --out benchmarks/backend_sweep_partialsb_2026-09-02.tsv
